# Notion MCP Safety & Quality

**This rule applies when Notion MCP is connected and the user is working with Notion pages, databases, data sources, comments, or views.**

## MCP & Data Safety

- Assume the workflow is executed via Notion MCP (`user-Notion`) and ensure the MCP is connected before running operations.
- Before writing to a page or data source, **always fetch schema first** via `notion-fetch`. Database schemas define required properties, property types, and available templates — writing without first inspecting the schema produces malformed pages or silent property misassignment.
- For pages outside a database, the only valid property is `title`. For pages inside a database, use the fetched data source schema's property names exactly (case-sensitive).
- Never create duplicate pages for the same semantic entity without first calling `notion-search` or `notion-query-data-sources` to confirm absence.
- When given a database URL, treat it as a `database_id` only if the database has exactly one data source. Multi-data-source databases require `data_source_id` resolution via `notion-fetch` on the database URL first.

## Tool Surface

**Read-only tools (`posture: fail-open`)** — safe to invoke without confirmation:

| Tool | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `notion-fetch` | Fetch a page, database, data source, or block by ID or URL |
| `notion-search` | Workspace-wide search (pages, databases, comments) |
| `notion-query-data-sources` | Structured query against a data source (filter, sort, pagination) |
| `notion-query-meeting-notes` | Query the user's meeting-notes data source |
| `notion-get-comments` | List comments on a page or discussion |
| `notion-get-teams` | Enumerate teams in the workspace |
| `notion-get-users` | Enumerate users in the workspace |

**Mutating tools (`posture: fail-closed`)** — require explicit user confirmation of payload before invocation:

| Tool | Mutation | Confirmation payload MUST include |
|---|---|---|
| `notion-create-pages` | Creates one or more pages | parent (page_id / database_id / data_source_id), full properties map, content (or template_id), icon + cover (if set). Confirm the full `pages[]` array — never invoke with an unreviewed batch. |
| `notion-update-page` | Updates page properties or content | page_id, every property being changed (before / after), content diff if any |
| `notion-duplicate-page` | Clones a page (with or without content) | source page_id, target parent, whether content is included |
| `notion-move-pages` | Moves pages to a new parent | page_ids[], new parent, confirmation that source parent is acceptable to leave |
| `notion-update-data-source` | Updates a data source's schema or properties | data_source_id, full schema delta (properties added / removed / renamed). Schema mutations cascade across every page in the data source; confirmation is MANDATORY. |
| `notion-create-database` | Creates a new database | parent, initial schema, title |
| `notion-create-view` | Creates a view on a data source | data_source_id, view type, filter / sort config |
| `notion-update-view` | Updates an existing view | view_id, filter / sort / visibility delta |
| `notion-create-comment` | Posts a comment | target page_id or discussion_id, full comment body, mentions (if any) |

A mutating call executed without the user's prior review of the payload is a safety violation. Schema mutations on data sources in particular require explicit confirmation because they cascade.

## Usage Guidance

- **Enhanced Markdown spec.** Before generating Notion page content, fetch the spec at `notion://docs/enhanced-markdown-spec` via `notion-fetch`. Notion-flavored Markdown differs from standard Markdown in toggles, callouts, embedded mentions, and property references. Do not hallucinate syntax.
- **Title discipline.** Never include the page title at the top of the page's `content` — set it in `properties.title` only. Duplicate titles are a common error pattern.
- **Property formatting for expanded types.** Date, place, number, and checkbox properties have expanded key formats (`date:{property}:start`, `place:{property}:latitude`, `"__YES__"` / `"__NO__"`, JavaScript numbers). Follow the tool description exactly; do not reformat.
- **Reserved property names.** Properties named `id` or `url` (case-insensitive) require the `userDefined:` prefix (`userDefined:URL`, `userDefined:id`). Unprefixed they collide with Notion's internal fields.
- **Templates.** When creating a page from a database template, pass `template_id` and omit `content`. Template application is asynchronous — the page appears immediately but template content populates shortly after.
- **Search scope.** Prefer `notion-query-data-sources` over `notion-search` when the target is known to live in a specific data source; structured queries are cheaper and more precise.

## Graceful Degradation

When a Notion tool is unavailable (network, auth, rate limit, permission denied):

- **Read-only tool failure** (`fail-open`): log the failure, surface the error, continue with already-known context. Do not block.
- **Mutating tool failure** (`fail-closed`): abort the containing turn. Do NOT retry automatically; surface the error and ask the user how to proceed. Particular care is warranted for `notion-update-data-source` failures — a partial schema mutation can leave a data source in an inconsistent state. On such failure, immediately re-fetch the data source via `notion-fetch` and report the current schema state to the user.
- **`permission_denied`**: report to the user that the integration lacks access to the target page or database; do not attempt to escalate via a different parent ID.
- **Rate limit**: respect Notion's `Retry-After` signaling; do not aggressively retry.

## Boundary

This rule governs Notion MCP only. Other Notion integrations (the public Notion REST API, unofficial wrappers) have distinct surfaces and require their own safety treatment if consumed.
